WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:05.050
Terminator: Dark Fate has screened for critics
— and while the franchise has left them

00:00:05.050 --> 00:00:08.300
cold in recent years, it looks like the trend
is about to be reversed.

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:11.940
"I'm back."

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:16.520
A good deal of the reviewers who took in the
film are comparing it favorably to Terminator

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:20.930
2: Judgment Day, and many also agreed that
the film will make a star out of Mackenzie

00:00:20.930 --> 00:00:21.600
Davis.

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:26.080
Linda Hamilton's return to the role of Sarah
Connor was welcomed, and the flick's intriguing

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:30.200
story and slam-bang action sequences were
also singled out for praise.

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:34.980
Having said that, we're cautiously optimistic
that the initial reactions to Terminator:

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:39.200
Dark Fate will hold up, because the flick
comes with a pretty darn sterling pedigree.

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:44.460
It functions as a direct sequel to T2, ignoring
all of the increasingly ridiculous sequels

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:45.660
that followed that film.

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:47.280
"Talk to the hand"

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:51.860
It also marks James Cameron's return to the
franchise as a producer, and it was helmed

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:56.330
by Tim Miller, the man who brought us the
piece of pure cinematic joy that was 2016's

00:00:56.330 --> 00:00:57.000
Deadpool.

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:01.680
Gabriel Luna has been well known among Marvel
circles since his inspired performance as

00:01:01.690 --> 00:01:06.210
Ghost Rider in Marvel's Agents of S.H.I.E.L.D.,
and fans are no doubt excited that he plays

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:08.360
a souped-up Terminator in this movie.

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:10.580
So what do the critics have to say about it?

00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:13.840
For starters, the folks at Collider were roundly
blown away by the flick.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.399
Editor-in-chief Steven Weintraub was among
the many praising the performance of Davis,

00:01:18.399 --> 00:01:22.890
who stars as a cyborg sent back in time to
protect a new "mother of the future" portrayed

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:24.000
by Natalia Reyes.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:27.920
Weintraub enjoyed the trifecta offered by
Davis, Reyes, and Hamilton.

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:28.420
He wrote

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:32.880
"[Terminator: Dark Fate] is easily the best
Terminator movie since T2.

00:01:32.899 --> 00:01:37.649
Has some fantastic action and loved how the
film features 3 female protagonists that all

00:01:37.649 --> 00:01:38.340
kick ass.

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:41.420
So awesome seeing Linda Hamilton back as Sarah
Connor."

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:45.660
In a followup tweet, Weintraub had a word
of recommendation for those unfamiliar with

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:46.520
Davis' work, writing:

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:50.700
"I've been a fan of Mackenzie Davis since
seeing her in Halt and Catch Fire.

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:55.100
But she takes it to another level in [Dark
Fate] where she kicks all kinds of ass.

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:58.760
If you like her work in Dark Fate, go back
and watch [Halt and Catch Fire]."

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:03.409
Collider editor Perri Nemiroff seemed a touch
surprised to be penning such a positive response,

00:02:03.409 --> 00:02:03.900
saying:

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:06.580
"Is [Terminator: Dark Fate] the best since
T2?

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:07.380
Yup.

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:12.920
Linda Hamilton's return makes a BIG difference,
Mackenzie Davis kicks all the ass, &amp; Natalia

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:14.620
Reyes is an excellent anchor.

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:16.980
Gabriel Luna's Rev-9 is a winner too.

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:20.240
Physicality, VFX, fights — all spot on!

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:21.900
Glad they gave this another go."

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:25.540
Their colleague, horror editor Haleigh Foutch,
offered a similar opinion:

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:26.580
"It's solid.

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:27.980
Super well articulated action.

00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:29.780
Linda Hamilton is aces.

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:32.100
Easily the best sequel since T2.

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:36.960
[Dark Fate] takes The Force Awakens' 'if it
ain't broke, don't fix it' mentality to Cameron's

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:38.700
films and turns out it ain't broke."

00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:43.730
Ms. Foutch was not the only observer to notice
an aesthetic similarity between Dark Fate

00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:47.340
and the movie that washed the bad taste of
the Star Wars prequels out of the mouths of

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:47.840
fans.

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:50.900
Gizmodo entertainment reporter Germain Lussier
wrote:

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:53.760
"[The movie is] basically Terminator: The
Force Awakens.

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:59.500
A satisfying, exciting reboot that's essentially
the best parts of T1 &amp; T2 in a blender.

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:04.960
Davis, Reyes &amp; Hamilton are excellent, the
action wows &amp; while it's familiar, it's easily

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:06.360
the best T3 yet.

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:07.740
Terminator is back."

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:12.900
Slashfilm owner-editor Peter Sciretta also
drew the comparison, although he did make

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:15.400
sure to point out that Miller is, well, no
Cameron.

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:17.960
"[Dark Fate] is better than you think it's
going to be.

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:22.500
It's trying hard to be the Force Awakens of
Terminator movies, and mostly succeeds.

00:03:22.510 --> 00:03:25.850
But it's lacking Cameron's innovation, evolution
and directing.

00:03:25.850 --> 00:03:30.060
Arnold is fantastic, the best of his post
T2 Terminator appearances."

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:34.340
Sciretta wasn't the only to sing the praises
of Arnie, who has certainly caught his share

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:38.760
of flak for continuing to show up in the Terminator
franchise well into his golden years.

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:43.180
Of course, there was also plenty of praise
to go around for Hamilton, who apparently

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:46.600
had no trouble slipping back into the skin
of her most famous character.

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:49.780
Uproxx senior entertainment writer Mike Ryan
said:

00:03:49.790 --> 00:03:51.750
"I was skeptical of [Dark Fate].

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:53.250
Thought the trailers were not great.

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:58.320
But I would up enjoying it quite a bit, and
the difference maker (vs the other post T2

00:03:58.320 --> 00:03:59.780
sequels) is Linda Hamilton.

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:03.520
It's always been HER story and Hamilton adds
the needed weight here."

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:06.740
Screencrush editor-in-chief Matt Singer agreed
wholeheartedly.

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:09.340
"[Terminator is] thought of as Arnold's franchise.

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:12.900
[Dark Fate] makes it clear he wasn't the key
ingredient that made it work.

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:14.000
It was Linda Hamilton.

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:16.960
She's incredible in this, and the movie's
very solid."

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:21.199
All in all, it was a pretty amazing outpouring
of love for a sequel that nobody was sure

00:04:21.199 --> 00:04:25.770
would work, and it seems to have accomplished
the impossible in making some critics actually

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:27.900
look forward to more Terminator movies.

00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:32.900
These initial reactions certainly seem to
confirm that Terminator: Dark Fate may finally,

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:37.780
at long last, be a Terminator movie that doesn't
come out of the gate having to justify its

00:04:37.780 --> 00:04:38.320
existence.

